<p>Someone leaves, you deactivate their Zoom account, and a month later the invoice hasn't changed. It's a common frustration for Zoom admins. It feels like the deactivated user is still holding the license. Usually they aren't. The seat is still on your plan, and that's a different problem with a different fix.</p>

<h2>Deactivating removes the license, not the seat</h2>
<p>Zoom's help center is clear about what deactivation does: "Deactivating a user will remove all licenses associated with a user." The user can't sign in, their settings, meetings and recordings stay on your account, and the license goes back to your pool.</p>
<p>What deactivation doesn't do is change your plan. You bought a number of licenses, and you pay for that number whether the licenses are assigned or not. After you deactivate someone, the license moves from "assigned" to "unassigned" and you keep paying for it.</p>
<p>Lowering the number on your plan is a separate step, and Zoom applies it on its own schedule: a reduction in license quantity "will take effect at the end of your current billing cycle, not immediately." On an annual plan, that can be up to a year after the person left.</p>
<p>So the cost you see isn't the deactivated user. It's an <strong>unassigned seat</strong>, and those build up quietly with every person who leaves.</p>

<h2>Check: are any deactivated users still Licensed?</h2>
<p>It's still worth a quick check, because not every account looks the way the documentation describes. Accounts with several plans, bundles, or users who were deactivated before a license change can leave loose ends.</p>
<ol>
    <li>Go to <strong>User Management → Users</strong>.</li>
    <li>Filter by status <strong>Deactivated</strong>.</li>
    <li>Look at the license column. Anyone still shown with a Zoom Workplace or Meetings license should be cleaned up.</li>
    <li>Check add-ons too: Webinar, Large Meeting and Zoom Phone licenses are assigned per user.</li>
</ol>
<p>Then look at the bigger number. On the billing page, compare how many licenses you bought with how many are assigned. The difference is what you're paying for and not using, whether it came from deactivations, old invites, or overbuying at the last renewal.</p>

<h2>Your options</h2>

<h3>1. Reassign the seat</h3>
<p>If you're hiring, this is the cheapest fix: give the unassigned license to the next new starter instead of buying one. Nothing changes on the bill, but you avoid adding to it.</p>

<h3>2. Reduce the license quantity</h3>
<p>If you don't need the seat, lower the count on your plan. In the web portal this is under <strong>Plan Management</strong> or <strong>Account Management → Billing</strong>, depending on your account. You need to be the owner, an admin, or have a role with billing permissions. You can only remove licenses that aren't assigned, and the change applies at the end of the billing cycle. If your account has custom pricing or a signed contract, you'll need to ask Zoom or your reseller instead.</p>

<h3>3. Delete the user, with a data transfer</h3>
<p>Deleting a user is permanent. Zoom removes "the user, including their settings, meetings, webinars, recordings, and more." Before you delete, Zoom lets you transfer their meetings, webinars, cloud recordings, clips, whiteboards, notes and docs to another user. Meetings scheduled with their Personal Meeting ID can't be transferred. Delete when you're sure nobody needs their data, or after you've moved it.</p>

<h3>4. Unlink the user</h3>
<p>Unlinking removes the user from your account and gives them their own free Basic Zoom account, with their settings, meetings and recordings. This suits contractors and people who used a personal email, not staff whose recordings belong to the company.</p>

<h3>If a deactivated user still shows as Licensed</h3>
<p>Change their license to <strong>Unassigned</strong> with <strong>Zoom Meetings Basic</strong> in the user's edit screen, or select several and use <strong>Change Licenses</strong>. If the portal won't let you change a deactivated user, reactivate them, downgrade, and deactivate again. Do it in one sitting so they aren't left active.</p>

<h2>Automating the clean-up</h2>
<p>The lasting fix is a routine rather than a one-off clean-up:</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Offboarding:</strong> add "check the license was released" to your leaver checklist, next to deactivating the account.</li>
    <li><strong>Monthly:</strong> compare purchased and assigned licenses. If the gap is growing, stop buying seats until you've used it up.</li>
    <li><strong>Before renewal:</strong> reduce the quantity to what you'll actually use, well before the renewal date. <a href="{{ route('blog.show', 'how-to-free-up-zoom-licenses') }}">Our guide to freeing up Zoom licenses</a> shows how to work out that number.</li>
</ul>
<p><a href="{{ route('home') }}">SeatTrim</a> tracks this for you. It shows purchased versus assigned seats, flags any deactivated user who still holds a license, and reminds owners and admins before renewal with the seat count to reduce to.</p>
